/P 1213 0 R
/E (Initial Testing: Begin with default PID settings, conducting hover and basic movement tests to detect instability or oscillations.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1214 0 R
/E (Proportional Gain Tuning: Gradually increase proportional gain for quick responses without oscillations. Reduce slightly if oscillations occur.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1215 0 R
/E (Integral Tuning: Adjusting the integral term to compensate for steady-state drift and provide a minor degree of lag but never ebbed instability.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1216 0 R
/E (Derivative Tuning: Change the derivative term to reduce oscillation, especially in situations with rapid changes in directions.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (Why PID Tuning is Important:)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (Without proper tuning, a drone would never be able to remain stable in a hover. Instead, it would bob, drift, and likely crash if it were in flight. Additionally, tuning increases the drones responsiveness of the drone, making it easier for the pilot to fly precisely. Overall, changes of the PID values and testing their impact has been important, and successful.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
